INT. SUDS-N-DUDS LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Humming dryers, orange plastic chairs bolted to the floor, a vending machine selling single-serve detergent. A TV on a bracket plays the weather, muted.

NADIA BELOV, 40s, scrubs under a parka, feeds quarters into a washer. Her shoes are still wet. TUCKER SHAW, 20s, in a hockey jersey, sits on a folding table with a cardboard cup of coffee, scrolling his phone. MARISOL OYELOWO, 30s, in a hoodie, dumps a bag of towels into the next machine.

NADIA
Is this one broken?

TUCKER
It eats quarters. But it works.

NADIA
Great.

MARISOL
Try hitting the side.

Nadia hits the side. The washer coughs and starts.

NADIA
Thanks.

MARISOL
Everybody does it.

Tucker sips. Looks at the TV. Snow, again.

TUCKER
They said it'd stop by nine.

MARISOL
They say a lot of things.

NADIA
It's ten thirty.

TUCKER
Then we're all in this together.

A silence. The dryers hum on.

The door chimes. WALTER DIBBLE, 80s, in pajama bottoms and a trench coat, shuffles in dragging a laundry bag like a body. He looks at the three of them, then at the machines, then at the sign.

WALTER
Is there a machine that takes dollars?

TUCKER
Just the change machine, sir.

WALTER
It's out of order.

MARISOL
It's been out of order for a year.

NADIA
I have quarters.

WALTER
I'd pay you back.

NADIA
It's fine.

She counts out eight quarters and hands them over. He holds them like a collection plate.

WALTER
Bless you.

TUCKER
You got somewhere to be?

WALTER
Only here.

He loads a machine. Closes the lid slowly, patiently, as if it were a casket. Starts it. The drum begins to turn.

NADIA
Is your wife--

WALTER
She's passed. Last spring. I never learned how.

MARISOL
It's not hard.

NADIA ^
It's not hard.

They say it together, same moment, same tone. They look at each other. Smile. Tucker slides off the table.

TUCKER
Hey, Walter. Come sit.

WALTER
I'm all right.

TUCKER
Come sit anyway.

Walter hesitates. Then he takes the plastic chair beside Marisol.

Nobody speaks. The machines turn. The snow keeps falling on the window. For a while, it's enough.
